   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m6"]},"explication":"**always** est un adverbe de fréquence : « toujours »."},{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m4","m15"]},"explication":"**well** et **fast** disent la manière. *Fast* garde la même forme comme adjectif et comme adverbe."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["well"]]},"affichage":"well","explication":"**well** : l’adverbe correspondant à *good* est *well*, jamais *goodly*."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["e","b","a","d","c"]},"explication":"**I always get up early.**"}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('7be97ad8-b4f5-5493-8d98-e0f8ac226e31'::uuid, 2, 2, 10, 35, 5,
   $ex${"titre":"Les habitudes de Sam","competence":"lire-tableau","situation":"Sam raconte à son correspondant combien de jours par semaine il pratique ses activités. Il faut trouver l’adverbe de fréquence qui convient à chaque ligne.","documents":[{"id":"habitudes","type":"tableau","titre":"How often does Sam practise?","colonnes":["Activity","Days a week"],"lignes":[["Piano","7"],["Football","5"],["Swimming","1"],["Cooking","0"]],"enteteLignes":true,"source":"Tableau écrit pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Associe chaque activité à l’adverbe de fréquence qui lui correspond.","aide":"Du plus fréquent au moins fréquent.","type":"association","gauche":[{"id":"g0","texte":"Piano (7 jours)"},{"id":"g1","texte":"Football (5 jours)"},{"id":"g2","texte":"Swimming (1 jour)"},{"id":"g3","texte":"Cooking (0 jour)"}],"droite":[{"id":"d0","texte":"often"},{"id":"d1","texte":"rarely"},{"id":"d2","texte":"never"},{"id":"d3","texte":"always"}]},{"enonce":"Touche **toutes** les cases qui montrent une activité que Sam fait **moins de deux fois par semaine**.","aide":"Cherche les nombres inférieurs à 2.","type":"zone","document":"habitudes","multiple":true},{"enonce":"Complète d’après le tableau.","aide":"Regarde la ligne *Cooking*.","type":"trous","segments":["Sam ",0," cooks."],"banque":["always","never","usually","often"]},{"enonce":"Range chaque phrase.","aide":"L’adjectif décrit un nom ; l’adverbe décrit un verbe.","type":"categories","categories":[{"id":"c0","texte":"Adjectif (décrit un nom)"},{"id":"c1","texte":"Adverbe (décrit une action)"}],"items":[{"id":"i0","texte":"He sings well."},{"id":"i1","texte":"It’s a fast car."},{"id":"i2","texte":"She is a careful driver."},{"id":"i3","texte":"He is a good singer."},{"id":"i4","texte":"It goes fast."},{"id":"i5","texte":"She drives carefully."}]},{"enonce":"Après *look*, on emploie un adjectif. Complète.","aide":"Après *look, seem, feel*, pas d’adverbe.","type":"trous","segments":["She looks ",0,"."],"banque":["sadness","sad","sadly","sadder"]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"association","paires":{"g1":"d0","g2":"d1","g3":"d2","g0":"d3"}},"explication":"**Always** 100 %, **often** ~70 %, **rarely** ~10 %, **never** 0 %."},{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["r2c1","r3c1"]},"explication":"**1** (swimming) et **0** (cooking) sont inférieurs à 2."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["never"]]},"affichage":"never","explication":"**Sam never cooks** : 0 jour par semaine. L’adverbe se place avant le verbe lexical."},{"cle":{"type":"categories","items":{"i0":"c1","i1":"c0","i2":"c0","i3":"c0","i4":"c1","i5":"c1"}},"explication":"L’**adjectif** décrit un nom (*driver, singer, car*), l’**adverbe** décrit une action (*drives, sings, goes*)."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["sad"]]},"affichage":"sad","explication":"**She looks sad** : après *look*, on emploie l’adjectif."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('7be97ad8-b4f5-5493-8d98-e0f8ac226e31'::uuid, 3, 3, 15, 50, 5,
   $ex${"titre":"L’article sur M. Jones","competence":"argumenter","situation":"Le journal de l’école publie un portrait de M. Jones, un professeur surmené. Le tableau qui l’accompagne rappelle comment former les adverbes.","documents":[{"id":"portrait","type":"texte","genre":"article","titre":"Mr Jones, a busy teacher","blocs":["Mr Jones works hard. He hardly sleeps these days, and he has been very tired lately. He drives carefully and he speaks politely to everyone."],"source":"Portrait écrit pour l’exercice"},{"id":"formes","type":"tableau","titre":"From adjective to adverb","colonnes":["Adjective","Adverb"],"lignes":[["slow","slowly"],["happy","?"],["simple","?"],["good","well"]],"enteteLignes":true,"source":"Tableau écrit pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche l’adverbe de **degré** qui renforce *tired*.","aide":"Il se place devant l’adjectif.","type":"zone","document":"portrait","multiple":false},{"enonce":"Associe chaque adverbe à son sens exact.","aide":"Attention : *hardly* et *lately* ne sont pas les adverbes de *hard* et *late* au sens attendu.","type":"association","gauche":[{"id":"g0","texte":"hard"},{"id":"g1","texte":"hardly"},{"id":"g2","texte":"late"},{"id":"g3","texte":"lately"}],"droite":[{"id":"d0","texte":"récemment"},{"id":"d1","texte":"dur, avec effort"},{"id":"d2","texte":"tard, en retard"},{"id":"d3","texte":"à peine"}]},{"enonce":"Complète les deux cases à trouver du tableau.","aide":"Les adjectifs en *-y* font *-ily* ; ceux en *-le* font *-ly*.","type":"trous","segments":["happy → ",0," ; simple → ",1]},{"enonce":"Remets en ordre l’ordre habituel : manière, lieu, temps. La phrase commence par She.","aide":"Manière, puis lieu, puis temps.","type":"ordre","items":[{"id":"a","texte":"sang"},{"id":"b","texte":"at the concert"},{"id":"c","texte":"beautifully"},{"id":"d","texte":"last night"},{"id":"e","texte":"She"}]},{"enonce":"Complète.","aide":"*Enough* se place après l’adjectif.","type":"trous","segments":["He is tall ",0," to play basketball."],"banque":["enough","very","so","quite"]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
   $ex$[{"cle":{"type":"zone","ids":["m13"]},"explication":"**very** est un adverbe de degré : il se place **devant** l’adjectif."},{"cle":{"type":"association","paires":{"g3":"d0","g0":"d1","g2":"d2","g1":"d3"}},"explication":"**Hardly** = à peine ; **lately** = récemment. Les adverbes de *hard* et *late* sont *hard* et *late*."},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["happily"],["simply"]]},"affichage":"happily · simply","explication":"**happily** (y devient i + ly) et **simply** (le + y)."},{"cle":{"type":"ordre","ids":["e","a","c","b","d"]},"explication":"**She sang beautifully at the concert last night.**"},{"cle":{"type":"trous","trous":[["enough"]]},"affichage":"enough","explication":"**tall enough** : *enough* se place **après** l’adjectif."}]$ex$::jsonb),
  ('791a8ecf-17f0-593e-a963-c5f318cff75b'::uuid, 1, 1, 5, 20, 3,
   $ex${"titre":"Que fait Lina en ce moment ?","competence":"comprendre-langue","situation":"Tom envoie un message à Lina pour savoir où elle est. Elle lui répond et lui décrit aussi ses habitudes.","documents":[{"id":"park","type":"dialogue","modele":"sms","titre":"Tom et Lina","participants":[{"nom":"Tom","emoji":"👦","cote":"gauche"},{"nom":"Lina","emoji":"👧","cote":"droite"}],"repliques":[{"qui":"Tom","texte":"Hi! Where are you?"},{"qui":"Lina","texte":"I’m at the park now. I’m playing with my dog. Usually I do my homework on Saturdays!"}],"source":"Messages écrits pour l’exercice"}],"questions":[{"enonce":"Touche le mot qui montre que l’action est **en cours**.","aide":"Il répond à « quand ? » : « en ce moment ».","type":"zone","document":"park","multiple":false},{"enonce":"Complète avec une habitude.","aide":"*Every day* annonce une habitude. N’oublie pas le -s.","type":"trous","segments":["She ",0," the piano every day."],"banque":["playing","is playing","plays","play"]},{"enonce":"Range chaque marqueur.","aide":"Habitude ou action en cours ?","type":"categories","categories":[{"id":"c0","texte":"Présent simple"},{"id":"c1","texte":"Présent en BE + -ING"}],"items":[{"id":"i0","texte":"always"},{"id":"i1","texte":"Look!"},{"id":"i2","texte":"on Mondays"},{"id":"i3","texte":"now"},{"id":"i4","texte":"every day"},{"id":"i5","texte":"at the moment"}]}]}$ex$::jsonb,
